<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Conquest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/2015">2015</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as George Robert Acworth Conquest</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Conquest was a British and American historian, poet and novelist, one of the leading Western Sovietologists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. After briefly joining the Communist Party as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> student, he served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and from 1948 to 1956 worked in the Foreign Office's anticommunist Information Research <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a>. His The Great Terror (1968), the first comprehensive study of the Soviet purges of 1936–1938, estimated that they caused some 20 million deaths; in 2008 he put the total at no lower than thirteen to fifteen million. The Harvest of Sorrow (1986) dealt with collectivisation and the famine that followed it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-conquest.jpg" alt="Robert Conquest" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob C. Croes (ANEFO) · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Conquest_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A decree of May 1929 defined a kulak household by hired labour, machinery, renting out equipment or trade; in <b>Robert Conquest</b>’s words, peasants with “a couple of cows or five or six acres more than their neighbors” were labelled kulaks.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Malvern, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Palo Alto, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.11,-2.33&amp;year=1980" aria-label="Malvern on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4498 364 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Conquest"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4948 634h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4948 634h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.11,-2.33&amp;year=1980">What was here in 1980? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
